Not necessarily—and I shouldn’t have implied that without reviewing the contract. **Stopping the wire, exercising an offset, and disputing an invoice may be treated differently.** Whether stopping it triggers default or acceleration depends on the exact clauses.

The key provisions are:

- payment due date and method;
- any **setoff/offset notice** requirement;
- what specifically constitutes a **default**;
- any notice and **cure period** before default remedies;
- the lender’s right to **accelerate/call the note**;
- clauses barring withholding despite a dispute;
- fraud, billing-error, or good-faith dispute provisions;
- waiver and governing-law language.

A 30-day notice requirement for an **offset** does not automatically mean the entire note can be accelerated the moment a payment is paused. The contract might require separate notice of default and time to cure—but some agreements define nonpayment as an immediate default, so the wording matters.

Because the decision is tonight and involves $25,000, don’t rely on a definitive interpretation without the document. **Send or upload the note/contract—especially the payment, offset, default, cure, and acceleration sections—and I can map out what it actually says.** Meanwhile, you can ask the bank whether the wire can be placed on a temporary administrative hold without characterizing it as an offset, but that does not guarantee protection from contractual default. A local contracts attorney is the safest source for a time-critical decision.
